    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        StudioBid needed to turn an auction API into a trustworthy product for
        discovering, evaluating, selling, and bidding on creative work. The
        interface had to explain changing values—such as the current bid, time
        remaining, and account credits—while keeping the core journey direct for
        both new visitors and returning users.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Auction interfaces combine urgency with financial decisions. Users
            need to know what an item is worth to them, whether it is still
            available, what others have bid, and whether they have enough
            credits before committing to an action.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Make current bids, time remaining, and listing status easy to
              scan</li>
            <li>Build trust through consistent hierarchy, spacing, and feedback</li>
            <li>Support many changing listing states without redesigning every
              screen</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/web-studiobid-600.webp"
            data-src="web-studiobid-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="465"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="StudioBid style guide showing the colour palette and Poppins typography scale"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">A restrained visual system for
            trustworthy auction interactions</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Structured listing cards around image, price, timing, and status
              so users can compare opportunities before opening a detail page
            </li>
            <li>
              Gave the current bid and primary action clear visual priority
              while keeping seller, description, and history available for
              evaluation
            </li>
            <li>
              Defined reusable cards, buttons, badges, fields, and feedback
              patterns so the product could scale across browsing, bidding,
              selling, and profile views
            </li>
            <li>
              Planned authenticated and unavailable states so protected actions
              explain what the user needs to do next
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/web-studiobid-ui-600.webp"
            data-src="web-studiobid-ui-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="513"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="StudioBid high-fidelity screens showing listing details and account creation on desktop and mobile"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Responsive high-fidelity screens for
            core auction and account flows</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block">
          <h2>From flow to system</h2>
          <p>
            Early wireframes helped separate profile activity into clear areas
            for active listings, bids, and wins. That structure became a
            repeatable dashboard pattern rather than a collection of unrelated
            account pages.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Mapped browse → listing details → bid as the primary customer
              journey</li>
            <li>Separated selling activity from bidding activity within the
              profile</li>
            <li>Adapted content density and controls for smaller screens</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/web-studiobid-wireframe-600.webp"
            data-src="web-studiobid-wireframe-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="481"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="StudioBid profile dashboard wireframe with tabs for active listings, bids, and wins"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Profile dashboard wireframe
            organising selling and bidding activity</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        StudioBid became a reusable UI system rather than a set of isolated
        screens. The hierarchy supports quick listing comparisons, more
        considered bidding decisions, and predictable account management across
        desktop and mobile. The project strengthened my ability to design for
        changing product states before those states are implemented in code.
      </p>
    </div>

